ssue as above. No obvious melds. So maybe not the best.\n\nPlay W5: I have two W5s. Playing one would leave me with W5, W7, T6, B4, B7, F3, J3. Let's see. If I have W5, W7, and maybe W6? But W6 is in the unshown tiles as 3. So if I have W5 and W7, maybe a Chow if I can get W6. But I don't have it. Alternatively, maybe form a Pung of W5 if I have another one. But I only have one left. So not helpful. Not sure.\n\nPlay F3: I have F3 in my hand. Playing it would leave me with W5x2, W7, T6, B4, B7, J3. Again, same problem as before. No clear melds.\n\nPlay W7: I have one W7. Playing it would leave me with W5x2, T6, B4, B7, F3, J3. Let's see. W5x2, T6, B4, B7, F3, J3. Maybe form a Pung of W5 (but only two), or maybe a Chow with W5, W6, W7. But no W6. Not helpful.\n\nPlay B4: I have one B4. Playing it would leave me with W5x2, W7, T6, B7, F3, J3. Again, same issue. No clear melds.\n\nPlay B7: I have one B7. Playing it would leave me with W5x2, W7, T6, B4, F3, J3. Again, same problem.\n\nHmm. All these plays don't seem to lead to an obvious winning hand. Maybe I need to look for other patterns. Let's check if my current hand can form a valid winning hand with the existing tiles.\n\nCurrent hand: W5x2, W7, T6, B4, B7, F3, J3. Melds: two [T5, T6, T7]. So total tiles: 2 (from W5) + 1 (W7) + 1 (T6) + 1 (B4) + 1 (B7) + 1 (F3) + 1 (J3) = 8 tiles. Plus the two melds (each 3 tiles) = 8 + 6 = 14. So the current hand is already a complete hand. Wait, no. Wait, the melds are two sets of three tiles each, so 6 tiles. The hand has 7 tiles. Wait, no: the hand is listed as [W5:2; W7:1; T6:1; B4:1; B7:1; F3:1; J3:1]. That's 2+1+1+1+1+1+1 = 8 tiles. Plus the two melds (each 3 tiles) = 6 tiles. So total 14 tiles. So the current hand is already a complete hand. Wait, but the melds are part of the hand. So the total is 6 (melds) + 8 (hand) = 14. But the hand is already the 14th tile. Wait, no. Wait, the hand tiles are the 14th tile. The melds are already formed. So the current hand is 8 tiles